@node	2.5.1, 2.5.2, 2.5, 2.5
@subsection Generic Arithmetic Operations

The task of designing generic arithmetic operations is analogous to that of
designing the generic complex-number operations.  We would like, for instance,
to have a generic addition function @code{add} that acts like ordinary
primitive addition @code{+} on ordinary numbers, like @code{addRat} on
rational numbers, and like @code{addComplex} on complex numbers.  We can
implement @code{add}, and the other generic arithmetic operations, by following
the same strategy we used in @ref{2.4.3} to implement the generic
selectors for complex numbers.  In the book, a type tag is attached to each
kind of number; in this edition the tag is the value's own runtime type, one
level of a @newterm{@code{sealed interface}} tower, and the generic function
dispatches to an appropriate package according to the types of its
arguments.

The generic arithmetic functions are defined as follows:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GenError>)
fun add(table: NumTable, x: Num, y: Num): Num = applyGeneric(table, "add", listOf(x, y))

context(r: Raise<GenError>)
fun sub(table: NumTable, x: Num, y: Num): Num = applyGeneric(table, "sub", listOf(x, y))

context(r: Raise<GenError>)
fun mul(table: NumTable, x: Num, y: Num): Num = applyGeneric(table, "mul", listOf(x, y))

context(r: Raise<GenError>)
fun div(table: NumTable, x: Num, y: Num): Num = applyGeneric(table, "div", listOf(x, y))
@end example

@noindent
Every fallible function of this section takes a @newterm{@code{Raise<GenError>}}
context parameter, the edition's typed replacement for the book's
@code{error} calls: a dispatcher that finds no handler, or a handler that meets
arguments of the wrong shape, raises a @code{GenError} instead of returning a
sentinel.

We begin by installing a package for handling @newterm{ordinary} numbers, that
is, the primitive numbers of our language.  The book tags these with the symbol
@code{scheme-number}; this edition wraps the host's own @code{Long} values as
@code{ZLong} tower values, which present the tag @code{integer}.  The
arithmetic operations in this package are the host's checked primitive
arithmetic (so there is no need to define extra functions to handle the
untagged numbers), though this package adds one thing the book's lacked: where
checked arithmetic runs past the @code{Long} width, the result promotes to
@code{BigZ} rather than wrapping around.  Since these operations each take two
arguments, they are installed in the table keyed by the list of tags
@code{listOf("integer", "integer")}:

@example kotlin
fun installIntegerPackage(table: NumTable) {
    table.put("add", listOf("integer", "integer")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoLongs(args)
        try {
            ZLong(Math.addExact(a, b))
        } catch (e: ArithmeticException) {
            BigZ(a.toBigInteger().add(b.toBigInteger()))
        }
    }
    table.put("sub", listOf("integer", "integer")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoLongs(args)
        try {
            ZLong(Math.subtractExact(a, b))
        } catch (e: ArithmeticException) {
            BigZ(a.toBigInteger().subtract(b.toBigInteger()))
        }
    }
    table.put("mul", listOf("integer", "integer")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoLongs(args)
        try {
            ZLong(Math.multiplyExact(a, b))
        } catch (e: ArithmeticException) {
            BigZ(a.toBigInteger().multiply(b.toBigInteger()))
        }
    }
    table.put("div", listOf("integer", "integer")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoLongs(args)
        if (b == 0L) raise(GenError.DivideByZero("integer div"))
        try {
            ZLong(Math.divideExact(a, b))
        } catch (e: ArithmeticException) {
            BigZ(a.toBigInteger().divide(b.toBigInteger()))
        }
    }
    table.put("make", listOf("integer")) { args ->
        val (a, _) = twoNums("make", args)
        a
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
Users of the integer package will create (tagged) ordinary numbers by means of
the function:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GenError>)
fun makeInteger(table: NumTable, n: Long): Num {
    val make = table.get("make", listOf("integer")) ?: r.raise(GenError.NoMethod("make", listOf("integer")))
    return make.invoke(r, listOf(ZLong(n)))
}
@end example

@noindent
Now that the framework of the generic arithmetic system is in place, we can
readily include new kinds of numbers.  Here is a package that performs rational
arithmetic.  Notice that, as a benefit of additivity, we can use without
modification the rational-number code from @ref{2.1.1} as the internal
functions in the package; the one concession this edition makes is to carry the
numerator and denominator as @code{BigInteger} values, since a @code{Long}
pair can overflow during multiplication before any reduction happens:

@example kotlin
fun installRationalPackage(table: NumTable) {
    fun Raise<GenError>.makeRat(
        n: BigInteger,
        d: BigInteger,
    ): QRat {
        if (d.signum() == 0) raise(GenError.DivideByZero("make-rational"))
        val g = n.abs().gcd(d.abs())
        val pair = if (d.signum() < 0) n.negate() to d.negate() else n to d
        return QRat(pair.first.divide(g), pair.second.divide(g))
    }

    fun Raise<GenError>.twoRats(args: List<Num>): Pair<QRat, QRat> {
        val (a, b) = twoNums("rational arithmetic", args)
        if (a !is QRat || b !is QRat) raise(GenError.BadArgs("rational arithmetic", "expected two rationals"))
        return a to b
    }
    table.put("add", listOf("rational", "rational")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoRats(args)
        makeRat(a.num.multiply(b.den).add(b.num.multiply(a.den)), a.den.multiply(b.den))
    }
    table.put("sub", listOf("rational", "rational")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoRats(args)
        makeRat(a.num.multiply(b.den).subtract(b.num.multiply(a.den)), a.den.multiply(b.den))
    }
    table.put("mul", listOf("rational", "rational")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoRats(args)
        makeRat(a.num.multiply(b.num), a.den.multiply(b.den))
    }
    table.put("div", listOf("rational", "rational")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoRats(args)
        makeRat(a.num.multiply(b.den), a.den.multiply(b.num))
    }
    table.put("make", listOf("rational")) { args ->
        val (n, d) = twoNums("make", args)
        if (n !is ZLong || d !is ZLong) raise(GenError.BadArgs("make", "expected two integers"))
        makeRat(n.n.toBigInteger(), d.n.toBigInteger())
    }
}

context(r: Raise<GenError>)
fun makeRational(table: NumTable, n: Long, d: Long): Num {
    val make = table.get("make", listOf("rational")) ?: r.raise(GenError.NoMethod("make", listOf("rational")))
    return make.invoke(r, listOf(ZLong(n), ZLong(d)))
}
@end example

@example kotlin
val table = NumTable()
installGenericArithmetic(table)
show(add(table, makeRational(table, 1, 2), makeRational(table, 1, 4)))
// => 3/4
@end example

@noindent
We can install a similar package to handle complex numbers, using the tag
@code{complex}.  In creating the package, we use the representation-level
constructors @code{repMakeFromRealImag} and @code{repMakeFromMagAng}, the same
ones the rectangular and polar packages of @ref{2.4.1} were built on.
Additivity permits us to use, as the internal operations, the same
representation-level functions from @ref{2.4.1} under their @code{rep}
prefixes: @code{repRealPart}, @code{repImagPart}, @code{repMagnitude}, and
@code{repAngle}.

@example kotlin
fun installComplexPackage(table: NumTable) {
    table.put("add", listOf("complex", "complex")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoComplex(args)
        Complex(repMakeFromRealImag(repRealPart(a.rep) + repRealPart(b.rep), repImagPart(a.rep) + repImagPart(b.rep)))
    }
    table.put("sub", listOf("complex", "complex")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoComplex(args)
        Complex(repMakeFromRealImag(repRealPart(a.rep) - repRealPart(b.rep), repImagPart(a.rep) - repImagPart(b.rep)))
    }
    table.put("mul", listOf("complex", "complex")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoComplex(args)
        Complex(repMakeFromMagAng(repMagnitude(a.rep) * repMagnitude(b.rep), repAngle(a.rep) + repAngle(b.rep)))
    }
    table.put("div", listOf("complex", "complex")) { args ->
        val (a, b) = twoComplex(args)
        Complex(repMakeFromMagAng(repMagnitude(a.rep) / repMagnitude(b.rep), repAngle(a.rep) - repAngle(b.rep)))
    }
    table.put("make-from-real-imag", listOf("complex")) { args ->
        val (x, y) = twoNums("make-from-real-imag", args)
        if (x !is Real || y !is Real) raise(GenError.BadArgs("make-from-real-imag", "expected two reals"))
        Complex(repMakeFromRealImag(x.d, y.d))
    }
    table.put("make-from-mag-ang", listOf("complex")) { args ->
        val (m, a) = twoNums("make-from-mag-ang", args)
        if (m !is Real || a !is Real) raise(GenError.BadArgs("make-from-mag-ang", "expected two reals"))
        Complex(repMakeFromMagAng(m.d, a.d))
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
Programs outside the complex-number package can construct complex numbers
either from real and imaginary parts or from magnitudes and angles.  Notice how
the underlying functions, originally defined in the rectangular and polar
packages, are exported to the complex package, and exported from there to the
outside world.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GenError>)
fun makeComplexFromRealImag(table: NumTable, x: Double, y: Double): Num {
    val make =
        table.get("make-from-real-imag", listOf("complex"))
            ?: r.raise(GenError.NoMethod("make-from-real-imag", listOf("complex")))
    return make.invoke(r, listOf(Real(x), Real(y)))
}

context(r: Raise<GenError>)
fun makeComplexFromMagAng(table: NumTable, mag: Double, ang: Double): Num {
    val make =
        table.get("make-from-mag-ang", listOf("complex"))
            ?: r.raise(GenError.NoMethod("make-from-mag-ang", listOf("complex")))
    return make.invoke(r, listOf(Real(mag), Real(ang)))
}
@end example

@noindent
What we have here is a two-level tag system.  A typical complex number, such
as @math{{3 + 4i}} in rectangular form, is a @code{Complex} value wrapping a
@code{Rect} of its parts, as shown in @ref{Figure 2.24}.  The outer level
(the @code{complex} tag the type presents) is used to direct the number to the
complex package.  Once within the complex package, the representation inside
(@code{rectangular} here) is used to direct the work to the rectangular code.
In a large and complicated system there might be many levels, each interfaced
with the next by means of generic operations.  In the book, a data object
passed ``downward'' has its outer tag stripped off (by applying
@code{contents}) so the next level of tag becomes visible; here nothing is
stripped, because the tags are the types themselves: each package's handler
destructures the wrapper it was installed for and works on the structure
inside.

