@noindent
In the above packages, we used @code{addRat}, @code{addComplex}, and the
other arithmetic functions exactly as originally written.  In the book's Lisp
these definitions become internal to different installation procedures and so
no longer need names that are distinct from each other: they could simply be
named @code{add}, @code{sub}, @code{mul}, and @code{div} in both packages.
This edition's top-level declarations cannot share one name, so the
representation-level internals keep the @code{rep} prefixes adopted in
@ref{2.4.1}.

@quotation
@strong{In this language: the type tag is the type.}
The book attaches a tag to every datum because every Lisp datum is a pair; here
the tower is a @newterm{@code{sealed interface}} whose levels -- @code{ZLong},
@code{QRat}, @code{Real}, @code{Complex} -- present themselves as their own
tags, and @code{applyGeneric}'s dispatch key is the list of runtime types its
arguments carry:
@example kotlin
typeTagOf(qr(3, 4))
// => rational
@end example
A miss raises @code{GenError.NoMethod} through the @code{Raise<GenError>}
context parameter rather than returning a sentinel; the book's tag-as-data
still wins wherever the dispatch key is not in the type at all, as in
exercise 2.92's variable ordering.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.77}Exercise 2.77:} Louis Reasoner tries to evaluate
the expression @code{magnitude(z)} where @code{z} is the object shown in
@ref{Figure 2.24}.  To his surprise, instead of the answer 5 he gets an error
message from @code{applyGeneric}, saying there is no method for the operation
@code{magnitude} on the types @code{(complex)}.  He shows this interaction to
Alyssa P. Hacker, who says ``The problem is that the complex-number selectors
were never defined for @code{complex} numbers, just for @code{polar} and
@code{rectangular} numbers.  All you have to do to make this work is add the
following to the @code{complex} package:''

@example kotlin
// following added to the complex package
table.put("real-part", listOf("complex")) { args ->
    val z = args.single() as Complex
    applyRepGeneric(reps, "real-part", z.rep)
}
table.put("imag-part", listOf("complex")) { args ->
    val z = args.single() as Complex
    applyRepGeneric(reps, "imag-part", z.rep)
}
table.put("magnitude", listOf("complex")) { args ->
    val z = args.single() as Complex
    applyRepGeneric(reps, "magnitude", z.rep)
}
table.put("angle", listOf("complex")) { args ->
    val z = args.single() as Complex
    applyRepGeneric(reps, "angle", z.rep)
}
@end example

Describe in detail why this works.  As an example, trace through all the
functions called in evaluating the expression @code{magnitude(z)} where
@code{z} is the object shown in @ref{Figure 2.24}.  In particular, how many
times is @code{applyGeneric} invoked?  What function is dispatched to in each
case?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.78}Exercise 2.78:} The internal functions in the
@code{integer} package are essentially nothing more than calls to the
primitive functions @code{+}, @code{-}, etc.  It was not possible to use the
primitives of the language directly because our type-tag system requires that
each data object have a type attached to it.  In fact, however, all Lisp
implementations do have a type system, which they use internally.  Primitive
predicates such as @code{symbol?} and @code{number?}  determine whether data
objects have particular types.  Modify the definitions of @code{type-tag},
@code{contents}, and @code{attach-tag} from @ref{2.4.2} so that our
generic system takes advantage of the host's internal type system.  That is to
say, the system should work as before except that ordinary numbers should be
represented simply as the host's own numbers rather than as pairs whose
@code{car} is the symbol @code{scheme-number}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.79}Exercise 2.79:} Define a generic equality
predicate @code{equ?} that tests the equality of two numbers, and install it in
the generic arithmetic package.  This operation should work for ordinary
numbers, rational numbers, and complex numbers.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.80}Exercise 2.80:} Define a generic predicate
